# Manuscript PDF page 149

                              THE BLADE



   On the sidewalk outside, in the gray light, with a delivery guy
rolling up a gate and a pigeon doing something unhygienic to a bagel,
Danny turned to me with the gauze on his ear and the lanyard still
around his neck.
   “I’m not going to tell them,” he said. “About the window. I’ll say my
headphones blew.”
   “Why?”
   “Because you came over.” He said it like it was the dumbest,
simplest fact in the world. “You came over in your socks. Nobody from
there’s ever come over.” He scratched his head. “And because you’re
the only interesting thing I’ve logged in seven weeks.”
   He handed me a thumb drive from his pocket.
   “I’m not supposed to,” he said. “Don’t call me before four.”
   Rafa was waiting at the café at seven. I don’t know how he knew. He
sat in the corner with a cortado he wasn’t drinking and watched me
come in with my hands hanging at my sides, and Manny behind me
with his slippers still on, and he didn’t say a word until I’d sat down
across from him.
   “Tell me what you wanted,” he said.
   “To make him stop.”
   “Who?”
   I took my time with it.
   “Not him,” I said.
   “Did it work?”
   “No.”
   Rafa nodded. That was all. He pushed the cortado across the table
toward me, and I drank it cold.
   Manny took the other chair.
   “You said you’d tell me before you carried something,” he said. “On
the roof.”
   “I didn’t carry it. I swung it.”
   “You called me after.”
   “I know.”




